\section{Why a separate file rather than R code}{

A worker has to find out what a project needs \emph{before} it runs any of that
project's code. If the file list lived in an R script, discovering it would
mean sourcing the script, which is the thing being decided about. So the
manifest is declarative and inert.
}

\section{The entrypoint}{

The entrypoint is sourced once per worker, with the project directory as the
working directory, and must define a function \code{run_job(row)}. It receives a
single-row data frame -- one row of whatever you passed as \code{jobs} -- and may
return anything that \code{saveRDS()} can store.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{run_job <- function(row) \{
  data.frame(id = row$id, estimate = simulate(row$n, row$seed))
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The entrypoint may \code{source()} its siblings by project-relative path, and a
variable \code{jobr_project_dir} holds the unpacked project's absolute path for
projects that need to read their own data files.
}

